WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
Common CSRF prevention mistakes are usually review and consistency problems.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.240
A state-changing GET request is one of the clearest findings because it makes an action look like a read.

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.980
Missing antiforgery validation on unsafe actions is another.

4
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.840
So is assuming that a login session or authorization rule proves request intent.

5
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.460
These controls are related, but they do not replace one another.

6
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.620
Relying only on CORS is also a mistake.

7
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:35.040
CORS controls browser cross-origin response reads, not user intent for state-changing requests.

8
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.940
SameSite-only designs need review too.

9
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:45.800
SameSite can reduce exposure, but it should not be the only protection for ASP.NET Core workflows that are better served by antiforgery validation.

10
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:55.120
Inconsistent protection across MVC, Razor Pages, APIs, and AJAX endpoints is common in growing applications.

11
00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:03.380
A visible form may be protected while a partial view, modal, JavaScript endpoint, admin helper, or import workflow is missed.

12
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.640
Teams should inventory state-changing browser endpoints rather than assuming one convention covers every path.

13
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.520
Local development shortcuts should not become production configuration.

14
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:24.980
Relaxed cookie settings, temporary CORS origins, disabled validation during testing, or one-off JavaScript submission patterns can remain long after the original reason is gone.

15
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:31.560
Production review should confirm that the deployed behavior matches the intended CSRF model.

16
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.480
Data Protection keys need planning across deployments and multiple instances.

17
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:46.900
If keys are not persisted or shared appropriately, antiforgery token validation can fail unpredictably when requests move between instances or after deployment changes.

18
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.120
Reliability matters because noisy false failures can pressure teams to weaken validation instead of fixing the root configuration issue.

19
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:08.240
A safe review identifies state-changing endpoints, credential behavior, antiforgery validation, SameSite cookie settings, CORS assumptions, Data Protection key behavior, failure logging, and documented exceptions.

20
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.380
The goal is not to find one magic setting.

21
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.120
The goal is a consistent model that the team can operate and explain.
